WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.527
Some systems deserve early attention because they are difficult to patch or replace.

2
00:00:05.527 --> 00:00:12.897
Legacy applications may depend on old libraries, unsupported protocols, or hard-coded algorithm assumptions.

3
00:00:12.897 --> 00:00:21.111
Embedded systems and IoT devices may have limited processing power, small memory, fixed firmware, or weak update mechanisms.

4
00:00:21.111 --> 00:00:28.634
Operational technology may have strict availability requirements and long certification or maintenance cycles.

5
00:00:28.634 --> 00:00:37.923
Medical devices, vehicles, satellites, industrial controllers, building systems, and field equipment can remain in service for many years.

6
00:00:37.923 --> 00:00:46.444
Their cryptography may be tied to boot validation, remote access, telemetry, updates, identity, or vendor maintenance channels.

7
00:00:46.444 --> 00:00:57.345
Even if a post-quantum replacement is available in software, the system may not have the capacity, protocol support, or operational window needed for quick migration.

8
00:00:57.345 --> 00:01:03.793
Cloud services and vendor products also create timelines outside direct organizational control.

9
00:01:03.793 --> 00:01:16.460
A company may rely on managed identity, SaaS applications, hardware security modules, key management services, certificate authorities, mobile device platforms, and third-party libraries.

10
00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:20.298
Quantum readiness therefore includes vendor management.

11
00:01:20.298 --> 00:01:29.203
Security teams need roadmaps, support statements, implementation timelines, validation expectations, and interoperability plans.

12
00:01:29.203 --> 00:01:33.425
The systems that are hardest to patch should be identified early.

13
00:01:33.425 --> 00:01:36.880
That does not mean replacing all of them immediately.

14
00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:45.094
It means documenting dependency, ownership, exposure, expected lifetime, update path, vendor status, and business impact.

15
00:01:45.094 --> 00:01:55.381
Long migration timelines are easier to manage when discovered before a deadline, and much harder when discovered after every other system is already waiting.
